<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Java</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> occupying the eastern part of the island of Java and the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, with its capital at Surabaya. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, whose capital lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trowulan">Trowulan</a>. From the 16th century it was absorbed by Muslim states such as Demak and later Mataram before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-java.jpg" alt="East Java" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Java_in_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in the easternmost third of the island of Java. Its only land border is with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> in the west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> lie along its northern and southern coasts, and the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali-strait">Bali Strait</a> to the east separates Java from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. The province also includes the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, the Kangean islands and the Masalembu archipelago. Its two main rivers are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a>, 290 kilometres long, and the Solo, 548 kilometres long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> has been inhabited since prehistoric times, as fossils found at Mojokerto, Trinil and Wajak show. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinoyo-inscription">Dinoyo inscriptions</a> found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malang">Malang</a>, dating from 760 CE, are the oldest written sources in East Java and tell of events in the Kingdom of Kanjuruhan. In 1222 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-arok">Ken Arok</a> founded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>, which he ruled until 1227. His descendants became kings of Singhasari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> from the 13th until the 15th century.</p><p>The date when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> is unclear. Some experts place it around the 11th century, on the evidence of the tomb of Fatimah Binti Maimun at Leran, dated to 1085 CE, which also shows that Arab traders frequented the north coast. Islam gradually supplanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> in the 14th and 15th centuries, and the last nobles and people of the fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> expedition of four ships under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-de-houtman">Cornelis de Houtman</a> in 1596 began the Dutch presence on the island, and by the end of the 18th century the Dutch had expanded their influence over the Islamic sultanates in the interior of Java.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is Surabaya, the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and a major industrial and business centre. East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> is Indonesia's second most populous province after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>. Most of the population is Muslim, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> communities among the Tenggerese and the Balinese. Indonesian is the official language, but Javanese and Madurese are most frequently used. The Suramadu Bridge connects Surabaya with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Java” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…core lands (nagara agung), whose rice fields were assigned as appanages to princes and officials; beyond them were the outer provinces (mancanagara) of eastern Central Java and <b>East Java</b>, and the coastal districts, each governed by regents (bupati) who sent tribute and attended the court at fixed times.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Sundanese text of the late 16th century, makes Sanjaya Sanna's son, tells how Sanna was defeated by Purbasora of Galuh and how Sanjaya avenged him and ruled West, Central and <b>East Java</b> and Bali; romanticised as it is, its agreement with the Canggal inscription suggests a historical core.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 928 and 1006 King Mpu Sindok moved the capital of the Mataram kingdom to <b>East Java</b> after a series of volcanic eruptions, and several sources take this as the most likely period of abandonment; Mpu Prapanca's Nagarakretagama, written in the Majapahit era around 1365, still mentions &quot;the…</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 930s Mpu Sindok moved the Javanese court to <b>East Java</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/prambanan">Prambanan · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Trenggana expanded inland and west, taking Sunda Kalapa from the Sunda Kingdom in 1527 and conquering much of <b>East Java</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1019 priests consecrated him king, and over the next two decades he reconquered <b>East Java</b> from rival lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li></ul></section>
